      "canonicalConceptId": "concept:1aa32fee2a0ebcc5a5f232dfdaabb92726585dd41c5d739e9e637a2b70dc7d19",
      "text": "The brain-first versus body-first model proposes that alpha-synuclein pathology in Parkinson's disease begins either in the brain or in the enteric or peripheral autonomic nervous system, and the proposers hypothesize that isolated REM sleep behaviour disorder is a prodromal phenotype of the body-first type. In their own review the proposers report that molecular imaging studies were generally in agreement with the model whereas structural imaging studies showed conflicting findings, a commentary notes limits on generalizability, and an autopsy preprint reports gut-restricted positivity in only a few subjects. Meta-analyses report gut microbiome alterations in Parkinson's disease, the authors of one state that differences in microbiome structure are small, and a later meta-analysis states that causality cannot be established. Two randomized fecal microbiota transplantation trials report different primary-outcome results.",
